"The patient underwent a robot-assisted radical prostatectomy with a pathological stage of pT3a, positive surgical margins, and no invasion of the seminal vesicles. The Gleason score was 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2). The absence of lymph nodes removed is noted, and lymphovascular invasion was absent. Biopsy findings showed a progression from Gleason 3+3 to 3+4 over 21.8 months. Preoperative PSA was 19.\n\nTwo primary factors drive the prognosis: the positive surgical margins and the higher Gleason grade of 3+4. Countervailing findings include the absence of lymph nodes removed and the absence of lymphovascular invasion. The lack of documented postoperative PSA or follow-up significantly widens the uncertainty regarding the trajectory. Furthermore, the nodal status is uncertain as no nodes were removed, and \"pNx is not pN0.\" Information regarding postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory is crucial for revising this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 63.96 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 41.58–86.35 months; its coverage has not been validated."
